This allows you to collect
metrics and build dashboards

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:50.220
for all token types, not just
completions and prompt tokens,

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.460
but also thinking,
reasoning, cash tokens,

00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:58.680
and other token types using the
LLM-emit-token-metric policy.

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:02.400
This policy forwards the token
metrics to Application Insights,

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:05.040
where you can set up alerts
or build more complete

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.920
and accurate dashboards
to represent token usage,

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:11.880
or maybe even cost of
consumption of models.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.640
I'm also excited to announce a
new capability that's available

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.630
now in preview called
Unified Model API.

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.990
This API creates a single
API in API Management

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:26.370
for various backend
model providers.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:29.790
For now, those backend providers
are Anthropic models using the

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.470
Messages API

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.460
and OpenAI-compatible models
using Chat Completions API.

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.250
You can have a client-facing
Chat Completions API interface

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.710
that automatically
translates calls

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.920
to Anthropic models
and to OpenAI models.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.950
This allows users to
standardize on a single SDK

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.650
or a single
client-facing interface,

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:54.150
while you can implement a lot of
different models on the backend.

00:14:54.150 --> 00:14:57.720
You can also use aliases, a
new functionality that's part

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:01.560
of this feature, to decouple
backend and client model names.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:05.550
For example, you can
create an alias called GPT

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:11.610
that will forward the request
to the GPT 5.3 backend.

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:15.300
But, with time, you can reswap
this alias, for example,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:19.170
to upgrade your model to
GPT 5.4, maybe swap it

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:21.900
for another model
provider like Anthropic

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:23.760
without affecting any clients.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:27.840
So clients will be able to just
use the areas while you have

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.260
full control over what's
actually the backend model

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:35.460
on the backend behind
the API Management.

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.320
This Unified Model
API also allows you

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:39.930
to centralize governance
of policies across models

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:41.250
from different providers.

00:15:41.250 --> 00:15:44.370
So you apply your policy
once, and it works for all

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:46.320
of these models on the backend.

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:48.630
You can also configure
failover across providers.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:50.490
So, for example,
you can failover

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:54.210
from an OpenAI GPT model to
an Anthropic Claude model,

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.000
even though there are
technically different API

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.160
formats on the back end,
but API Management handles

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.350
that translation for you.

00:16:01.350 --> 00:16:03.930
Lastly, I'm also excited to
announce general availability

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:05.640
of Bring Your Own
Model functionality

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.530
in Microsoft Foundry.

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:11.340
This capability allows you
to add any model proxied

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:14.700
by AI Gateways, such as
Azure API Management

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:20.790
or third-party gateways that is
Chat Completions API compatible,

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:23.580
and use that model for
building prompt agents

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:24.990
in the Foundry Agent Service.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:29.160
You can learn more about this
functionality at the link below.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.950
And with that, I would like to
hand it over to Anish to talk

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:35.880
about tool governance
in API Management.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:36.810
ANISH TALLAPUREDDY:
Thank you, Mike.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.900
Excited for these new launches.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.610
Now let's look into
tool governance

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:43.500
within API Management.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:44.610
Before we get started,

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.250
let's talk about what
exactly we define as tools.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:51.600
Tools are APIs or MCP
servers that you use

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:55.020
within your enterprise,
basically to drive action.

00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:58.320
AI models and LLMs, they
provide the intelligence.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:02.520
But in order to drive action,
you need to have these agents

00:17:02.520 --> 00:17:06.990
and LLMs connect to these tools,
such as APIs and services,

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:09.720
to turn that intelligence
into actions.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:12.620
But this creates a new
set of challenges.

00:17:12.620 --> 00:17:15.350
Enterprises, for the
last couple of decades,

00:17:15.350 --> 00:17:19.640
have built a huge sprawl
and portfolio of APIs

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:21.290
and various kinds of tools.

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:25.590
How do they repurpose this
into the new world of AI?

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:28.470
API Management could help solve
for that particular problem,

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.420
which we will dig
into in a second.

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:33.690
The more tools you add,
the more risk you create.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.000
In fact, the risk grows
exponentially according

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:37.170
to lots of surveys.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.560
So where every tool you add, the
potential security, compliance,

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.900
and performance challenges
increase exponentially.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:45.030
So how do we solve
for these problems?

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.250
Let's double-click into that.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:50.010
AI Gateway, just like
how it is the bridge

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.860
to mediate model usage,
it is also the bridge

00:17:52.860 --> 00:17:54.660
to mediate tool usage.

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:58.290
We solve for the same issues,
such as governance, security,

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:01.170
observability, velocity,
and scalability,

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:04.350
when you are interacting with
various kinds of tool backends,

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:06.480
like I mentioned before, APIs.

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:08.580
It could be cloud
services or connectors.

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:12.300
Here, I have just listed a few
examples, such as logic apps,

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:16.980
functions, container apps,
etc. You could also want

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:21.120
to mediate access to third-party
MCP servers natively

00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:23.220
and apply the same set
of governance policies

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:26.730
that you are applying to your
first-party APIs and services.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.400
You could talk to any kind
of third-party MCP server,

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:34.440
such as Atlassian, Stripe,
Box, Neon, or anything else.

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:39.840
So let's look at what can you do
with Azure API Management today.

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:41.790
One, you can create MCP servers.

00:18:41.790 --> 00:18:45.810
You can convert your existing
portfolio of REST APIs

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:48.630
into MCP servers in a
matter of a few clicks.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:49.830
No rewrites.

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:53.700
You can just expose any HTTP
backend as an MCP server in less

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.280
than a minute, in a
matter of a few clicks.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:00.240
You can also proxy
existing MCP servers,

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.180
either first-party MCP
servers that you're hosting

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:06.630
or third-party MCP servers
hosted by another party,

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:10.140
third-party, like we just
talked about, Stripe or Jira

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.890
that you might be using in your
organization, and proxy them

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:17.040
with this particular gateway
to apply the same governance

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.920
and the same security perimeter.

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:24.090
In terms of securing MCP
servers, after you create,

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.360
the next big challenge becomes
how do I secure these endpoints

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.980
to make sure agents and LLMs
accessing these endpoints are

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:35.670
authenticated, and we make sure
that they have the right access

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:39.060
and scopes to access these
particular MCP servers?

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:43.760
And similarly, we also make
sure how do we streamline

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.430
and simplify the access
to these MCP servers,

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:48.770
which might have their
own auth mechanism,

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:51.380
such as API keys or OAuth?

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:54.740
All of those things are
supported using capabilities

00:19:54.740 --> 00:20:00.360
such as credential manager and
policies within API Management.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:02.520
The third part is,
okay, you have created

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:04.170
and you have secured
these MCP servers.

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:05.940
Now I want to apply
various kinds

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:08.310
of organizational policies.

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:12.150
I might want to apply
content safety policies

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:15.000
where I don't want to
leak any PII information.

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.340
I want to apply
throttling policies

00:20:17.340 --> 00:20:23.310
so that I will make sure
there is no abuse of my tools

00:20:23.310 --> 00:20:25.410
by various kinds of
agents and LLMs.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:27.210
So you can apply all
kinds of policies,

00:20:27.210 --> 00:20:28.440
and all those are supported

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:31.980
for all these MCP endpoints
and tool endpoints.

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:34.320
Lastly, observability
for every call.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.020
Anytime you try to call
a particular tool,

00:20:37.020 --> 00:20:38.850
you want clear logs, traces,

00:20:38.850 --> 00:20:42.270
and metrics to understand
what exactly is happening

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:44.490
so that you can root
cause issues as well

00:20:44.490 --> 00:20:48.270
as improve the performance
of your applications

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.420
and the agentic workflows
that you're building.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:53.880
So it's the same
gateway, same policies.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.280
Whether it's a REST
client or an agent

00:20:56.280 --> 00:21:00.600
or an MCP-aware call coding
tool, we are able to support all

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:03.840
of these capabilities
within API Management.

00:21:03.840 --> 00:21:05.730
So let's talk about what's new.

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:09.570
We recently shipped more
capabilities for MCP servers

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:12.780
and tools within API Management.

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:16.320
So one of the first things we
recently shipped was the ability

00:21:16.320 --> 00:21:20.640
to add MCPs to the product
concept within API Management.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:23.610
This enables you to bundle
one or more MCP servers

00:21:23.610 --> 00:21:27.810
into API Management products,
subscriptions, quotas,

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.490
and approval workflows.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:34.110
It gives you the ability
to create specific bundles

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:38.190
so that you can specify the
specific set of curated rules

00:21:38.190 --> 00:21:39.930
for those particular products.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:42.900
You can also apply subscriptions
and quotas and various kinds

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:45.450
of approval workflows
for those products.

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:50.250
Similarly, we also got a
lot of asks from customers,

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:53.700
the ability to A-B test
these kinds of MCP servers

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.040
that are being created
within API Management.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.710
So we enabled MCP versions.

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:02.070
So you can create multiple
versions of an MCP server

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.530
so that you can run
various kinds of testing,

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:08.610
experimentation, or even
have one version in prod

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:10.110
and one version in deployment

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:14.250
and start making changes
simultaneously to test

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:19.080
and to improve your
development workflow.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.530
Then we also further improved
the overall observability

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:27.030
with more fine-grained data
and metrics for MCP tools.

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:30.030
It's more about having
metrics, traces,

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.880
and logs in open telemetry.

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:37.770
And we are able to track
end-to-end metrics for a request

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.660
from the time it
originates from a client

00:22:39.660 --> 00:22:42.480
to the time it hits
API Management gateway

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:45.330
to the time it goes to a
particular MCP server,

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.340
to understand which
tool got invoked,

00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:50.580
what auth mechanism got
invoked, etc. You have access

00:22:50.580 --> 00:22:52.680
to all of this information.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:57.660
And finally, we also improved
our automation capabilities

00:22:57.660 --> 00:23:02.250
by making MCP a first-class
element in management API,

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:04.320
our REST API for API Management.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:08.910
So you can use this automation
to directly create MCPs,

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:12.540
secure MCPs, and apply policies
directly through the REST API

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:17.700
and not having to go through the
Azure API Management portal.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:19.590
So let's look
through a quick demo

00:23:19.590 --> 00:23:21.270
of the new capabilities
we launched.

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:23.820
It should be a very quick
demo so that you understand

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:25.500
where these capabilities
are located

00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:28.560
and how you can leverage them.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:31.590
So this is my API
Management portal.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:33.990
And this is a basic V2 instance.

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.000
As you can see in the TOC on
the left side, I've clicked

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.860
on "MCP Servers"
within AI Gateway.

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:41.670
You can see a bunch of MCP
servers already there.

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:43.680
I'm trying to create
an MCP server.

00:23:43.680 --> 00:23:46.470
I'm using an
existing MCP server,

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.260
a third-party MCP
server, mcp.stripe.com.

00:23:49.260 --> 00:23:52.260
I've added a display
name and a base path,

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:54.090
and I'm also assigning
it to a product.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:57.030
So you can see, during the
creation flow, any time,

00:23:57.030 --> 00:24:00.330
you can assign a particular MCP
server to a particular product,

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:01.950
and it gets assigned
to that product.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:03.390
And then you can
go to the product

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:07.140
and start making all kinds of
changes that you want to do.

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:09.930
So that is the first
feature we launched.

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:10.980
The second thing you can see

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:13.440
that once the MCP
server got created,

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:17.160
you can apply various
kinds of policies.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:19.110
And you also have settings there

00:24:19.110 --> 00:24:23.130
where you can apply subscription
keys to authenticate access

00:24:23.130 --> 00:24:25.230
to this particular MCP server.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:26.670
Let's apply a policy.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:30.240
I will just apply a very
simple access policy

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:32.460
so that we understand that, hey,

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:34.800
we can access this
particular MCP server.

00:24:34.800 --> 00:24:38.520
Here I'm just posting
a particular key.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:41.160
It is just hard-coded
here just for simplicity.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:44.280
We also launched a new test
console within API Management.

00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:47.340
You don't need to go to an
MCP Inspector outside or spin

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.260
up your own MCP Inspector.

00:24:49.260 --> 00:24:51.690
The test console will
automatically enable you

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:53.670
to test the MCP server created.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:55.680
It is listing all
the tools here,

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:58.500
and you can invoke them
directly from there.

00:24:58.500 --> 00:25:00.870
And lastly, like I
mentioned, versioning.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:02.040
We also have the ability

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:05.640
to version MCP servers
that you create.

00:25:05.640 --> 00:25:07.380
So here I'm creating
a V2 version

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:12.190
of the MCP test build
2026 MCP server.

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:15.870
As you can see here,
I have two versions.

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:18.210
There is the original, and
there is the V2 version

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:21.480
with two particular private
endpoints that I could use

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:23.310
for experimentation,
A-B testing,

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:26.460
blue-green deployments, etc.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:28.350
So we have another demo.

00:25:28.350 --> 00:25:31.110
This would be quite
interesting, I think.

00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:35.880
We also have enabled the
ability to use API Management

00:25:35.880 --> 00:25:38.670
for tool selection at
agent design time.

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:42.930
So Mike, my colleague,
has created a good demo

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.520
to showcase this capability.

00:25:44.520 --> 00:25:46.950
Let's walk through that now.

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:48.630
MIKE BUDZYNSKI: Let me
show you a quick prototype

00:25:48.630 --> 00:25:51.990
of how AI Gateway simplifies
agent tool discovery

00:25:51.990 --> 00:25:52.890
at design time.

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:57.060
I'll start with a simple intent:
"I want to build an agent

00:25:57.060 --> 00:26:00.510
that calculates shipping
costs for online orders."

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:02.070
From that intent alone,

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:06.120
GitHub compiled queries
AI Gateway's MCP server

00:26:06.120 --> 00:26:07.860
to discover available tools.

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.590
It automatically selects
the tools that matter,

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:12.870
such as order lookup, into US,

00:26:12.870 --> 00:26:15.270
and international shipping
cost calculation,

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:17.970
and intelligently excludes tools

00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:20.340
that don't contribute
to the goal.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:22.710
I review and confirm
the selected tools.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:26.040
And Copilot generates the full
agent code using the GitHub

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:27.960
Copilot SDK.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.480
This isn't just a
basic scaffold.

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:32.760
It includes the correct
tool definitions

00:26:32.760 --> 00:26:35.280
and chaining logic
derived entirely

00:26:35.280 --> 00:26:37.830
from tool metadata
and descriptions.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:40.740
At this point, the agent
is ready to run locally

00:26:40.740 --> 00:26:43.140
or be deployed
directly to Foundry.

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:44.790
So how did we get here?

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:48.780
First, a lightweight custom
skill connects GitHub Copilot

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:52.350
to AI Gateway, enabling
Copilot to discover tools

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:55.170
and generate the agent
code automatically.

00:26:55.170 --> 00:26:58.860
Second, the GitHub
Copilot CLI is configured

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:01.320
with the AI Gateway's
MCP server,

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.870
which acts as the
discovery endpoint

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:07.890
for all tools exposed
through AI Gateway.

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:10.470
For this prototype, AI Gateway,

00:27:10.470 --> 00:27:12.930
which will combine the
capabilities of API Management

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:16.830
and API Center into a
Foundry-native, multi-tenant,

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:21.120
always-on component, is mocked
with standalone API Management

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.010
and API Center resources.

00:27:23.010 --> 00:27:26.070
API Center exposes
the MCP server

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.530
with all the tools
automatically synchronized

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:29.940
from API Management.

00:27:29.940 --> 00:27:32.580
Tools that don't match
the developer's intent,

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:36.300
like weather forecasting
in this case, are filtered

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:39.630
out by Copilot during discovery.

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:42.480
API Management facilitates
the tool's runtime

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:46.380
and enables conversion of
existing enterprise REST APIs,

00:27:46.380 --> 00:27:48.930
such as order lookup,
shipping cost calculation,

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:52.170
and weather forecasting,
into MCP servers.

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:54.630
Finally, we are also
actively prototyping more

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:56.220
advanced capabilities.

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:59.160
This includes AI Gateway
dynamically selecting tools

00:27:59.160 --> 00:28:03.120
at the agent's runtime, not just
at design time, and ensuring

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:06.420
that all model calls are
routed through AI Gateway

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:08.340
to enforce governance policies,

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.340
such as token
consumption limits.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:13.530
ANISH TALLAPUREDDY: Okay,
that's all on tools governance.

00:28:13.530 --> 00:28:16.050
Let me invite my colleague
Sreekanth to walk

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:20.310
through the enterprise
catalog for AI assets.

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:23.010
Sreekanth, over to you.

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:25.140
SREEKANTH THIRTHALA:
Thank you, Anish.

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:26.010
And we're going to talk

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:30.510
about the enterprise
catalog for AI assets.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:33.090
As enterprises scale
their AI investments,

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:36.180
one question keeps coming up:
How do we manage all of it,

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:39.150
the agents, the skills, the
MCP servers, and models?

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:42.180
How do teams discover what
exists and ensure quality

00:28:42.180 --> 00:28:43.770
and prevent duplication?

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:46.290
That's exactly what
Azure API Center solves.

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:49.170
It's the enterprise catalog
for all your AI assets,

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:51.660
a single system of record
for the enterprise

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:55.590
where every tool an agent might
call is registered, tracked,

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:58.620
and discoverable across
your organization.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:03.180
The catalog spans across
four asset types: REST APIs;

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:07.410
MCP tools, skills, plugins;
models; and agents.

00:29:07.410 --> 00:29:09.600
Keeping the catalog
current is effortless.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:13.380
You can register via portal,
the CLI, or sync directly

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:15.300
with your GitHub repo.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.600
There is no manual drift,
and assets ship as

00:29:18.600 --> 00:29:20.910
and when new commits are made.

00:29:20.910 --> 00:29:24.600
Quality assessment also is
provided for all these assets,

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:28.650
using LLM as a judge to evaluate
your skills for clarity,

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:31.470
parameter design, and
safety so developers

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:34.860
and agents can trust
what they consume.

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:39.000
And for the agentic world, API
Center serves as the marketplace

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.000
for agents, exposing a
marketplace endpoint

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:45.660
that enables developer tools
like Claude Code and Copilot CLI

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:48.180
to discover and load
enterprise skills, plugins,

00:29:48.180 --> 00:29:51.720
and MCP servers directly
from the catalog.

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:54.240
With the same lifecycle
and quality signals,

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.880
your platform teams curate.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:00.090
All of this works across
Azure, other clouds,

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:03.150
on-premises, and
third-party SaaS.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:05.070
One catalog, every asset.

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:07.950
Let's see a demo
of it in action.

00:30:07.950 --> 00:30:10.140
SPEAKER 1: API Center is
the enterprise catalog

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:13.740
for all your AI assets,
including skills, plugins,

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:15.420
MCP tools, and agents.

00:30:15.420 --> 00:30:17.370
Let us start with
registering skills.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:20.850
Click on "Register a Skill"
in the Asset sidebar menu.

00:30:20.850 --> 00:30:23.190
Provide details of the
skill's capabilities,

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:25.320
use cases, and behavior.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:27.660
You can also enter the
Git repository URL

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:28.770
for the skill source code.

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:31.260
In this example, we added
a new code review skill

00:30:31.260 --> 00:30:35.400
to the API Center asset catalog
and provided the Git repo URL

00:30:35.400 --> 00:30:36.870
of the Skill Markdown file.

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:38.910
With skills registered,
let's look at how

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.710
to keep them up
to date at scale.

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:43.050
To automate skill
registration and updates,

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:46.050
integrate a Git repository
with your API Center.

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:47.670
Any skills added or updated

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.340
in the repository are
automatically synchronized

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:54.060
with your API Center inventory,
eliminating manual updates

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:56.220
and keeping your
catalog always current.

00:30:56.220 --> 00:31:00.060
In this demo, we have integrated
the Azure Skills Git repository

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:03.510
to show how seamlessly external
skill sources can be connected

00:31:03.510 --> 00:31:05.580
and kept in sync
with API Center.

00:31:05.580 --> 00:31:07.680
Once the Git repo
is synchronized,

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.770
Azure API Center will
automatically import all Azure

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:13.890
skills as assets, making
them easily discoverable

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:15.240
across the enterprise.

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:18.240
API Center portal provides
an easy way for developers

00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:20.580
to discover skills
and its capabilities.

00:31:20.580 --> 00:31:23.070
Let us try to find the
Azure cloud migrate skill

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:25.650
from the Azure Skills Catalog,
which we just imported

00:31:25.650 --> 00:31:28.110
through the Git
repository integration.

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:30.930
Azure cloud migrate skill is
essential for agents looking

00:31:30.930 --> 00:31:34.110
to assess and migrate
cross-cloud workloads to Azure.

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:35.160
Clicking on the skill name

00:31:35.160 --> 00:31:38.580
in Azure API Center portal
opens the skill's definition,

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:41.670
including its reference
documentation, common tasks,

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:43.470
and its defined
inputs and outputs.

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:45.900
Further, developers can
contribute new skills

00:31:45.900 --> 00:31:46.890
by committing directly

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:49.830
to the Git repository
integrated with API Center.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:52.680
And once pushed, those skills
are automatically synchronized

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:55.440
with the catalog and made
available to the entire team

00:31:55.440 --> 00:31:58.000
without any manual intervention.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:03.150
Let us now talk about
skills assessment.

00:32:03.150 --> 00:32:06.630
AI skill assessments using
the LLM-as-a-judge technique

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:09.960
leverage a large language
model to evaluate AI outputs

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:13.290
against defined quality
criteria, scoring responses

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:17.130
across dimensions like accuracy,
coherence, and helpfulness.

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:20.370
The judge model can be prompted
with rubrics, reference answers,

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:23.520
or pairwise comparisons,
enabling scalable feedback

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:25.920
at a fraction of the cost
of human annotation.

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:29.100
API Center comes with default
skill assessment criteria

00:32:29.100 --> 00:32:31.080
out of the box,
evaluating skills

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:35.040
across four key dimensions,
each scored on a 1 to 5 scale

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:37.380
with a default threshold of 3.

00:32:37.380 --> 00:32:41.070
Documentation Clarity evaluates
how clearly a skill's purpose

00:32:41.070 --> 00:32:42.990
and behavior are communicated.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.720
Help Completeness assesses
whether the output serves

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:48.600
as a comprehensive
standalone reference.

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:51.630
Discoverability measures how
easily functionality can be

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.490
navigated and found.

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:56.820
Safe Usage evaluates whether
sufficient guidance is provided

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:58.290
for safe operation.

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:00.600
Enterprise platform
administrators can further

00:33:00.600 --> 00:33:01.950
extend these defaults

00:33:01.950 --> 00:33:04.440
by defining custom
assessment criteria tailored

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.870
to their organization's
specific standards,

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:09.630
compliance requirements,
and governance policies.

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.610
Once skills assessment
is enabled,

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:14.880
developers can view a detailed
AI quality score report

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:18.750
for each skill directly
within the API Center portal.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:20.910
This report provides
an at-a-glance pass

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.550
or fail status along with
per-dimension scores

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:25.230
and actionable feedback.

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.450
Alongside the LLM-based scores,

00:33:27.450 --> 00:33:29.640
the report includes
structural checks,

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:32.760
verifying foundational elements
like valid front matter,

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:36.240
skill name, and body content,
and schema validation,

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:37.890
which flags missing sections

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:39.900
such as examples
or error handling.

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:42.660
As a developer, this means you
can quickly understand the

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:46.230
quality and reliability of
a skill before adopting it,

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:49.230
making informed decisions about
which skills are ready to use

00:33:49.230 --> 00:33:51.810
and which may need
further refinement.

00:33:51.810 --> 00:33:54.930
Let us now transition
into MCP tools.

00:33:54.930 --> 00:33:58.950
As the MCP ecosystem grows,
organizations need a scalable,

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:02.100
automated way to register
and manage MCP tools

00:34:02.100 --> 00:34:03.780
without manual overhead.

00:34:03.780 --> 00:34:05.970
To automate MCP
tool registration

00:34:05.970 --> 00:34:07.800
and keep your
inventory up to date,

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:11.250
you can integrate a Git
repository with your API center,

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:14.730
eliminating manual onboarding,
and ensuring your catalog stays

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:16.740
in sync as tools evolve.

00:34:16.740 --> 00:34:17.700
Any changes pushed

00:34:17.700 --> 00:34:20.100
to the repository are
automatically reflected

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:23.040
in the catalog, giving teams
a single source of truth

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:25.920
for all MCP tools across
the organization.

00:34:25.920 --> 00:34:28.110
In this demo, we
showcase this end-to-end

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:31.410
by importing the Azure MCP
server into API Center

00:34:31.410 --> 00:34:32.850
through Git repo sync,

00:34:32.850 --> 00:34:35.880
demonstrating how quickly a
real-world MCP server can go

00:34:35.880 --> 00:34:38.160
from a repository to
a fully registered,

00:34:38.160 --> 00:34:42.090
discoverable asset
in your catalog.

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:44.310
The API Center portal
enables developers

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:47.970
to discover MCP servers and
interact with MCP tools

00:34:47.970 --> 00:34:50.000
by listing and running
them directly.

00:34:50.000 --> 00:34:51.980
Through the built-in
MCP Inspector,

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:55.460
developers can explore available
tools, inspect their inputs

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:57.890
and outputs, and test
them in real-time,

00:34:57.890 --> 00:35:00.300
all without writing a
single line of code.

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:04.230
In this example, the API Center
Data plane MCP server lets

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:07.830
developers list every MCP tool
in the catalog and search

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:10.910
for a specific server,
making it easy to evaluate

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.310
and integrate the right
tool into their workflow.

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:14.990
Now let us talk about plugins.

00:35:14.990 --> 00:35:16.910
Plugins are
self-contained extensions

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:20.250
that supercharge any supported
AI development tool,

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:24.270
adding custom commands, agents,
hooks, and MCP integrations

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:27.300
to Copilot CLI, Claude
Code, and beyond.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.550
Here we are on the
plugin registration form

00:35:29.550 --> 00:35:31.440
in Azure API Center.

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:34.830
We start by providing a title,
summary, and description

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:37.920
to make the plugin discoverable
and meaningful to developers

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:39.900
and agents browsing the catalog.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:42.900
We then specify a version to
track releases over time.

00:35:42.900 --> 00:35:45.930
What makes this powerful is
the ability to bundle skills

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:48.930
and MCP servers directly
within the plugin.

00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:51.630
So consumers get a single,
self-contained asset

00:35:51.630 --> 00:35:55.350
that is not just discoverable,
but immediately actionable.

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:58.950
With just a few fields, the
plugin is registered, governed,

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:02.340
and ready to be consumed by
agents, Copilots, and developers

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:05.040
across your organization.

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.080
The API Center portal
gives developers

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.990
and agents a single
place to browse, search,

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:14.640
and discover plugins alongside
APIs, skills, and MCP servers,

00:36:14.640 --> 00:36:16.590
eliminating scattered
repositories

00:36:16.590 --> 00:36:17.940
and tribal knowledge.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:21.570
Each plugin surfaces its
bundled skills and MCP servers

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:24.780
in one unified view, giving
consumers everything they need

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:28.510
to evaluate and connect with
the right asset quickly.

00:36:29.550 --> 00:36:32.280
Now let's look at how the API
Center Data plane brings it

00:36:32.280 --> 00:36:33.750
all together.

00:36:33.750 --> 00:36:36.390
API Center exposes a
marketplace endpoint

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:39.690
through its Data plane API,
surfacing plugins directly

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:43.730
to Claude Code in Copilot CLI,
making discovery seamless

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:45.860
without leaving your
development environment.

00:36:45.860 --> 00:36:48.890
Register the endpoint with
Copilot CLI in one step,

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:52.020
and the full plugin catalog
is instantly unlocked.

00:36:52.020 --> 00:36:55.680
From there, browsing, selecting,
and plugging in happens right

00:36:55.680 --> 00:37:00.270
from the CLI, keeping workflows
fast and friction-free.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:01.410
SREEKANTH THIRTHALA:
Let's now transition

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:04.470
into agent governance.

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:07.500
As agents proliferate,
raw, unmediated access

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.420
to backends doesn't scale.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:13.800
Users, apps, agents, and
Copilots are all making calls.

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:17.880
And A2A, REST, and HTTP
backends spanning any cloud

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:19.320
and on-premises.

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.900
You need something in the
middle that you can trust,

00:37:21.900 --> 00:37:24.380
and that's the AI Gateway.

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:29.180
It delivers five things:
governance, scalability, safety,

00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:32.060
and security, observability,
and velocity,

00:37:32.060 --> 00:37:36.060
one bridge for every
agent fully governed.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:37.770
And now for an announcement.

00:37:37.770 --> 00:37:40.950
Agent-to-agent support in Azure
API Management is generally

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:42.270
available today.

00:37:42.270 --> 00:37:46.200
A2A is becoming the backbone
of multi-agent architectures.

00:37:46.200 --> 00:37:49.950
And starting now, you can
bring your A2A agents

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:53.460
under the same enterprise-grade
management you rely

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:54.810
on for REST APIs.

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:58.680
Same governance, same security,
and same observability.

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:02.340
Today, we are GA-ing governance,
security, and observability

00:38:02.340 --> 00:38:07.050
across A2A agent APIs
alongside all your other APIs.

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:10.440
You can expose agent cards
and runtime operations.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:14.730
You can log open telemetry
traces using GenAI semantic

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:18.660
conventions for deep and
standardized visibility.

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:22.170
You can apply policies like
throttling, IP filtering,

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:25.170
and content safety
to every agent call.

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:29.520
And you can inventory
and publish APIs.

00:38:29.520 --> 00:38:33.960
As you can see, registering an
A2A agent is now very simple,

00:38:33.960 --> 00:38:36.450
with agent-specific
metadata like agent card

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:39.870
and agent URL surfaced
natively alongside lifecycle

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:41.430
and deployment details.

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:44.820
A2A support is now
generally available.

00:38:44.820 --> 00:38:45.900
And that's a wrap.

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:48.930
Thank you for attending the
Azure API Management session

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:50.580
at Microsoft Build today.

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:53.370
What we showed you today
is all available for you

00:38:53.370 --> 00:38:55.290
to use and for you to build.

00:38:55.290 --> 00:38:59.520
More details on it can be
found at aka.ms/apim/blog.

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:01.800
We can't wait to see
what you will build

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:03.750
with Azure API Management.

00:39:03.750 --> 00:39:04.520
Thank you.

